Security: Stop the federation secret exchange from leaking the stored token

When two trusted servers start the exchange of a shared secret at the same time,
the tie is broken by comparing the two tokens; the server with the greater token
wins and answers 403. The endpoint is public, so comparing the tokens in clear
text turned that answer into an oracle over the stored token: an unauthenticated
caller could submit chosen tokens and recover the stored one by binary search. The
tie is now broken over the SHA-256 hashes of the two tokens, which keeps the
outcome deterministic without revealing anything about the clear text.

Note for federated setups: the tie-break has to yield the same winner on both
sides. A server running this version and a peer that still compares the clear text
can therefore disagree, in which case both sides answer 403 and the exchange of
the shared secret does not complete. Establish trust with peers running the same
version, or connect them again once the peer has been updated.
